  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nEarly morning radar imagery indicates the final showers and \nembedded thunderstorms are exiting eastern Idaho into western \nWyoming. While high-resolution convective-allowing models (CAMs)\nmaintain low-end shower chances through daybreak, precipitation\nchances will diminish rapidly across the forecast area. \nWidespread sunshine will overspread the region by midday as a \ncooler, drier post-frontal air mass builds in. Daytime maximum \ntemperatures will top out in the upper 60s to mid-70s, making \nfor a pleasant late-August afternoon. The post-frontal surface \npressure gradient will support breezy afternoon winds of 15 to \n20 mph with gusts up to 30 mph, particularly across the upper \nSnake Plain and Arco Desert. Dry conditions will prevail \ntonight. Clear skies and efficient radiational cooling will \ndrive overnight lows into the 40s across lower elevation \nvalleys, while high-altitude basins fall into the 30s.\n\nEastern Idaho will remain under the influence of a broad upper-\nlevel trough anchored over the Pacific Northwest through early \nnext week. Multiple waves of shortwave energy embedded within \nthis southwesterly-to-westerly flow will cross the region. The \nfirst impulse tracks across the area on Monday, bringing \nisolated to scattered shower and thunderstorm chances primarily \nto the eastern half of Idaho. A secondary, more robust shortwave\napproaches western Idaho on Tuesday, elevating precipitation \nprobabilities across the Central Mountains and northward toward \nthe Montana Divide. Afternoon maximum temperatures will hold \nnear seasonal norms in the 70s to lower 80s.\n\nBy mid-to-late week, global ensemble guidance locks a broad\nupper-level cyclone near Vancouver Island, keeping persistent\ncyclonic flow across the Intermountain West. Periodic shortwave\nimpulses rotating around the parent low will track through the\nPacific Northwest. Current guidance suggests the bulk of deep\natmospheric moisture will stay shunted north and west of eastern\nIdaho from Wednesday through Friday, though localized mountain\nshowers remain possible. Model variance increases significantly\nregarding the exact strength, track, and timing of these\nindividual shortwaves. These subtle spatial differences will\nultimately dictate local daily wind speeds, cloud shading, and\nprecipitation coverage. Tightening pressure gradients ahead of\nincoming impulses will likely result in several breezy afternoon\nperiods. For now, National Blend of Models (NBM) guidance\nmaintains afternoon temperatures at or a few degrees below\nseasonal averages through next weekend.",
